Chapter: DiskFactsUnpackLarge
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts

 unpack-text is how a stored work's source comes back off the disk, so its
 cost is the repository's cost. It appended to the accumulator Text on every
 byte until 2026-08-14, which reallocates and copies the whole accumulator
 each time, and it now collects the characters into a list and joins them
 once.

 THE SIZE IS MEASURED AND THE OBVIOUS SIZE IS WRONG. cdx-serve died with OUT
 OF MEMORY serving a 64 KB work and served 48 KB in 23 s, so 64 KB looks like
 the cliff and is not: the old body reaches 64 KB comfortably in isolation and
 passes every check this arm makes. It failed THERE because that heap was
 already carrying the work index and the transport's receive buffer, and the
 quadratic accumulator only had to supply the last of the arena. An arm sized
 from that symptom would have been green against the very defect it was
 written for, which is how a test comes to certify nothing.

 Measured instead, with the old body deliberately restored: it survives 64 KB,
 and dies at 256 KB. So the arm is 256 KB, which is also the first power of
 two above the largest chapter in this tree (TypeChecker, 186,205 bytes). The
 fixed body runs it in under a second.

 Sabotage confirmed 2026-08-14: with the pre-2026-08-14 body in place this
 file answers OUT OF MEMORY and prints none of its four lines.

Section: Fixtures

  n : Integer = 262144

 The buffer is allocated ONCE in opening and threaded, not bound at module
 level. A module-level binding here is a RECIPE and not a cell: every
 reference runs alloc-bytes again, so a fill loop that named it would
 allocate 256 KB per byte and exhaust the arena before unpack-text was ever
 reached. The first draft of this file did exactly that and blamed the
 subject.

 A repeating 26-character cycle rather than a constant, so a body that
 returned the right LENGTH of the wrong characters is still caught, and one
 that dropped or duplicated a byte shifts every probe after the drop.

  fill : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fill (buf) (i) =
   if i >= n then 0
   else let p = poke-byte buf i (65 + int-mod i 26)
   in fill buf (i + 1)

  expect-at : Integer -> Integer
  expect-at (i) = 65 + int-mod i 26

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let buf = alloc-bytes n
    in let f = fill buf 0
    in let t = unpack-text buf 0 n
    in let len = text-length t
    in act
      print-line-uni ("length   : " & show len)
      print-line-uni ("first ok : " & (if char-code-at t 0 == expect-at 0 then "yes" else "no"))
      print-line-uni ("middle ok: " & (if char-code-at t (n / 2) == expect-at (n / 2) then "yes" else "no"))
      print-line-uni ("last ok  : " & (if char-code-at t (n - 1) == expect-at (n - 1) then "yes" else "no"))
    end
  end

